Welcome to the pen testing labs for this years CTF.

Please read the following carefully:

Rules
-----

- You MUST NOT under any circumstances attempt to access any systems
  other than the ones listed further on in this document. Any attempt
  to do so may constitute an offence under the Computer Misuse Act
  1990 or other relevant legislation or internal policy.

- The labs are deliberately not connected to the internet as they
  contain vulnerabilities. You MUST NOT connect them to the internet.

- The labs are built using docker images and all are solvable. You
  must solve them in the intended manner. Any attempt to inspect or
  otherwise obtain the flags without exploiting the configured
  vulnerabilities is cheating and may result in disqualification.

Now, with the boring stuff out of the way, here is the fun stuff.

Targets
-------

You have 3 "machines" (in reality docker containers), each with a
specific vulnerability and a flag to get once you break in. Each target
is more difficult than the last so I suggest you start with the first
one and work your way up.

As you may have noticed, your machine is called attacker.

Below are the IP addresses for all devices, you are all on the same
internal network.

attacker - 10.5.0.10
victim_1 - 10.5.0.11
victim_2 - 10.5.0.12
victim_3 - 10.5.0.13

Remember: ONLY TARGET THESE ADDRESSES!

Shells
------

You are currently in a bash shell. You may find it beneficial to have
multiple attacker shells open at once. If you want another shell, open a
new terminal on your lab machine and run the new-shell.sh script. You
will now have a second shell.

IMPORTANT: Do not close your original shell (the shell that opened when
you ran the start script). If you do, the labs will be reset.


Tools
-----

You have a limited number of tools available to you along with some
standard Linux commands:

 - nano (A text editor)
 - vim (Another text editor)
 - nmap (https://nmap.org/docs.html)
 - nmapAutomator.sh (https://github.com/21y4d/nmapAutomator)
 - nc (https://en.linuxportal.info/manuals/n/nc-traditional-linux-parancs)

If you need any help with anything, just ask one of the CTF organisers.

Have fun! :)
